#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343774 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e15de1-ee72-55ea-accb-1124f3a0ac0c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c138c60-0e79-52a2-81d0-4b4e52979618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-315" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c138c60-0e79-52a2-81d0-4b4e52979618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-315" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343776} :answer-field/id #uuid "72f223c2-bc17-5ad4-b864-5a8b149ef4b6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343775} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96f1ee1c-b8be-5f5e-96f3-7118f165b315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-225" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96f1ee1c-b8be-5f5e-96f3-7118f165b315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-225" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343778} :answer-field/id #uuid "62fc90e3-2472-59bf-b8fd-2ea4dc823356" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343777}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6b802eb6-631d-5681-82bc-9b27cb408bff" :question/math-academy-id "q-275008" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the exact solutions to the equation $3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+\\sqrt{8}=5\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ for $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘},$  are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+\\sqrt{8} & =5\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\ -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{8} \\\\ -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{4\\cdot 2} \\\\ -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we have to find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\text{arcsin}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNote that $x={45}^{∘}$ is not in the required domain $-{360}^{∘}\\le x\\le {0}^{∘}.$ To get a solution that is inside the required domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=x-{360}^{∘}={45}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{315}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/assets/504427bf4d324547e1a8b142a2fce58818b32491269ce676533672a0762903b1.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={135}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x={135}^{∘}$ is not in the required domain $-{360}^{∘}\\le x\\le {0}^{∘}.$ To get a solution that is inside the required domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={135}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{225}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to the equation, in ascending order, are \n\n$$\nx={-315}^{∘},\\,x={-225}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343779 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9ccf6b5-e087-5f7b-88e3-3767efbdcc26" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d9030fd-b24a-5dc3-a7fb-e505b9b375ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343781} {:answer/id #uuid "8a90b2e4-3112-56c6-945c-a7295641f25c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a90b2e4-3112-56c6-945c-a7295641f25c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343782} :answer-field/id #uuid "8abcf6ad-7188-54a8-9ee1-8f337540bf37" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343780} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77e3f637-be2f-5c38-8491-fa55fabe408d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "131" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77e3f637-be2f-5c38-8491-fa55fabe408d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "131" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343784} :answer-field/id #uuid "3779421a-b23f-52f4-917b-4778f35cbc63" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ff04ba2c-fbfa-541b-9cac-381cebf30bfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-338750" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions to the equation  $5\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=8-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$   are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =8-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\ 6\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =8 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})=(48.590\\,377…)^{∘}≈{48.590}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${48.590}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={48.590}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={48.5904}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={48.5904}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/assets/b6754300e279bf09409a49aeca7160a319ee90b5a120fe7ac775f12a8444b8af.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{48.590}^{∘} \\\\  & ={131.410}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈{49}^{∘},\\,x≈{131}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343785 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e15de1-ee72-55ea-accb-1124f3a0ac0c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d4ee799-1c78-5f27-b55d-e236b18761f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d4ee799-1c78-5f27-b55d-e236b18761f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343787} :answer-field/id #uuid "793a62c8-1c0f-5d0f-b6fb-7a09618d8f66" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343786} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc89e7f1-847d-5fbd-811a-c4094cfa009b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc89e7f1-847d-5fbd-811a-c4094cfa009b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343789} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4e7624f-c7ca-50bb-93a5-84eb23deae51" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "16429b6e-d61d-5e9f-88ca-c72fc8d47b41" :question/math-academy-id "q-338793" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation  $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{5}{3}$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{3}{5})=-0.643\\,501…≈-0.6435\n$$\n\nSince $-0.6435$ lies in the required domain, the first solution is ${x}_{1}=-0.6435.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.6435.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.6435.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/assets/d6fbe829a100e0ee59872f9bc8d2d6501e88b389ada63008fbe1ef6e6eee7ee4.png)\n\nSince the second solution lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.6435 \\\\  & ≈3.7851\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=3.7851$ is not in the required domain $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi .$ To get a solution that is inside the required domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=3.7851-2\\pi ≈-2.4981\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx=-2.5,\\,x=-0.6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80449399-3ae4-593f-8c3b-00bcdcc99727" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Outside of the Range of Cosecant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f52df8bd-c4fa-53af-b323-b939d24e97a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343792} {:answer/id #uuid "fa7a5b08-0a48-59c7-a4a9-ebe1ee71faff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343793} {:answer/id #uuid "6fb72772-f14e-562b-90f0-ac613ccfa5e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343794} {:answer/id #uuid "dd9e6910-0a5b-5ae9-8f28-78be1a83e250" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343795} {:answer/id #uuid "b289b840-df4f-529e-b0c4-7cbfe5f9219d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd9e6910-0a5b-5ae9-8f28-78be1a83e250" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343795} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab90d8df-c62d-542f-a6f0-cde3eec13ce0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af549981-340d-5010-bb51-ad7e4b14003c" :question/math-academy-id "q-66007" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-1=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=11\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-10$\n- $3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=1-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "We first simplify the given equations, and get\n\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-1$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=1$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{4}$\n\nNow, recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343797 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80449399-3ae4-593f-8c3b-00bcdcc99727" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Outside of the Range of Cosecant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dea97b21-a670-5603-ab75-5ea8994ccc17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343799} {:answer/id #uuid "1231550b-ef1b-5cdf-bd27-5636a3a5961b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343800} {:answer/id #uuid "a4b8d4a6-872e-52bc-8c9f-ba87cd6a8b47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343801} {:answer/id #uuid "761796d9-e681-5a4e-adad-3abb47cddebb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343802} {:answer/id #uuid "0061913e-4cb5-534e-b146-8aa1b0ecea57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dea97b21-a670-5603-ab75-5ea8994ccc17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343799} :answer-field/id #uuid "773586a5-2f91-511e-992b-a8cdaa0fe6ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45adcc56-6a37-5c2b-b6dc-99fe8cc12473" :question/math-academy-id "q-77809" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=12$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{7}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equations I and III have no solutions."}]}
